  <section class="lp-section" id="market">
    <div class="lp-wrap">
      <div class="lp-head"><span class="lp-kicker">The Dubai market, in numbers</span><h2 class="lp-title">Two audiences, one booking calendar: <span class="hl">residents and visitors</span></h2></div>      <div class="lp-market">
        <div class="lp-prose"><p>Dubai ended 2025 with 4.58 million residents, a 7.5% rise in a single year, according to the Dubai Data and Statistics Establishment. That is roughly 330,000 new people in twelve months, all looking for a new hairdresser, nail tech, laser clinic or dermatologist. The practical effect for your salon or clinic: every month a fresh wave of residents has no loyalty yet, and they choose almost entirely through Google Maps, reviews and Instagram.</p> <small>Source: Dubai Data and Statistics Establishment, reported by The National, July 2026.</small> <h3>The daytime city is far bigger than the census</h3> <p>The same statistics office puts Dubai's peak daytime population at 6.39 million, including about 1.81 million temporary visitors in town for work or tourism. On top of that, the Department of Economy and Tourism counted 19.59 million international overnight visitors in 2025. Many of them book a blow-dry, nails, lashes or a facial during their stay, usually from a phone, often late at night, and often within walking or short taxi distance of their hotel. Campaigns that only target “people who live in Dubai” miss this audience entirely.</p> <small>Sources: Dubai Data and Statistics Establishment (via The National, 2026); DET 2025 visitor report (via Zawya, 2026).</small> <h3>Search happens in more than one language</h3> <p>DET's 2025 visitor mix shows why: Western Europe supplied 21% of visitors, while the CIS &amp; Eastern Europe, South Asia and the GCC each supplied about 15%, and the wider MENA region 11%. A Russian-speaking visitor searching for a manicure, or an Arabic-speaking family from the Gulf looking for a ladies' salon, does not type the English keywords your competitors bid on. We build separate ad groups, landing copy and Google Business Profile content for the languages your district actually hears. See our pages on <a href="/russian-beauty-salon/">marketing a Russian-speaking beauty salon</a> and our <a href="/salon-marketing-dubai-guide/">complete guide to getting more salon bookings in Dubai</a>.</p> <h3>What this changes in your marketing</h3> <p>For a Dubai business, budget planning has to follow both curves: steady resident demand, and visitor demand that rises and falls with hotel occupancy (80.7% on average across 2025, per DET). In practice that means always-on local SEO and Maps work for residents, plus flexible paid campaigns around hotels, malls and beach districts that you scale up when visitor demand is strong. If you want to see what that paid layer costs, read <a href="/salon-instagram-ads-cost-dubai/">what a salon booking really costs on Instagram ads in Dubai</a>.</p></div>
