  <section class="lp-section lp-section--soft" id="rules">
    <div class="lp-wrap">
      <div class="lp-head lp-head--center"><span class="lp-kicker">Who regulates beauty in Dubai</span><h2 class="lp-title">DHA, DHCR or Dubai Municipality: <span class="hl">know whose rules your ads follow</span></h2><p class="lp-intro">In Dubai, the regulator depends on what you offer and where you are licensed. A hair salon in Al Barsha and a filler clinic in Dubai Healthcare City answer to different authorities, so their marketing has different limits.</p></div>      <div class="lp-rules lp-rules--3">
                <article class="lp-rule">
          <span class="lp-rule__tag"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l7 3v5.5c0 4.4-3 8.2-7 9.5-4-1.3-7-5.1-7-9.5V6z"/><path d="M8.8 12.2l2.2 2.2 4.2-4.4"/></svg>Clinics &amp; medspas</span>
          <h3>Dubai Health Authority (DHA)</h3>
          <div class="lp-rule__body"><p>DHA licenses healthcare facilities and professionals in Dubai through its Sheryan system. Its Standards for Medical Advertisement Content on Social Media (September 2026) apply to licensed facilities, practitioners, staff and influencers. Key points:</p> <ul> <li>Content that names or identifies a facility needs the facility medical director's approval before posting</li> <li>Banned wording includes “the best”, “safest”, “100%”, “no side effects”, “guaranteed success” and “immediate results”</li> <li>Before/after images must show the same person, with no editing, and state that results vary</li> <li>Written patient consent is needed for any photo, video or testimonial</li> </ul> <small>Sources: DHA Sheryan licensing portal (dha.gov.ae); Gulf News and Communicate Online, September 2026.</small></div>
        </article>
                <article class="lp-rule">
          <span class="lp-rule__tag"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l7 3v5.5c0 4.4-3 8.2-7 9.5-4-1.3-7-5.1-7-9.5V6z"/><path d="M8.8 12.2l2.2 2.2 4.2-4.4"/></svg>Dubai Healthcare City</span>
          <h3>DHCR (Healthcare City Authority – Regulation)</h3>
          <div class="lp-rule__body"><p>Clinics licensed inside the Dubai Healthcare City free zone follow DHCR's own advertising policy. It covers print, video, press releases and social media posts, and requires an Advertising Review Request at least 14 working days before an ad runs.</p> <p>Approved ads must carry their approval number. Ads displayed outside the facility also need MOHAP approval after DHCR has approved them.</p> <small>Source: DHCR Advertising Policy (dhcc.ae).</small></div>
        </article>
                <article class="lp-rule">
          <span class="lp-rule__tag"><svg viewBox="0 0 24 24" aria-hidden="true"><path d="M12 3l7 3v5.5c0 4.4-3 8.2-7 9.5-4-1.3-7-5.1-7-9.5V6z"/><path d="M8.8 12.2l2.2 2.2 4.2-4.4"/></svg>Salons &amp; beauty centres</span>
          <h3>Dubai Municipality</h3>
          <div class="lp-rule__body"><p>Salons, barbershops, beauty centres and mobile salons follow Dubai Municipality's technical guideline DM-HSD-GU77 (version 4, issued January 2025), from the Health and Safety Department.</p> <p>This matters for marketing because the guideline bars salons from giving medical advice or offering unauthorised medical services. Non-surgical cosmetic services in a salon also need a permit under DHA requirements. So a salon ad should not promise treatments the licence does not cover.</p> <small>Source: Dubai Municipality, DM-HSD-GU77 Technical Guidelines for Compliance of Salons, Barbershops and Beauty Centers, V4 (dm.gov.ae).</small></div>
        </article>
              </div>
      <p class="lp-note">Every Glowth clinic campaign in Dubai is written and reviewed against the rules that apply to your licence before it goes live. For the full playbook, read our <a href="/clinic-marketing-uae-guide/">guide to compliant aesthetic clinic marketing in the UAE</a>. This summary is general information, not legal advice; confirm specifics with your regulator.</p>    </div>
  </section>

